      ],
      "mergeID": "62d1a556e0d427519debc09c",
      "mergedPostDetails": "Documents folder is not a great place for configuration files that are not meant for users to see/delete/etc.  The better place for this is the appdata folder.\n\nEDIT: In response to the findability of the json file, it's literally linked directly in the settings page.",
      "mergedPostTitle": "[FEEDBACK, 0.2.0] Change default configuration directory to appdata instead of documents.",
      "parentID": "628d228cb624bb0b623b14da",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "628422e5905abe0b7107aa61",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5de9405aea7cc52772fcc359|full_name}: Technically, there is little difference between Documents and AppData. It's similar to difference between Documents, Pictures and Videos, etc. They're just folders and have different purpose. But in some cases, Documents may be synced by cloud storages such as OneDrive (I think it's Windows 10's default, but not tested). So current Cache/Logs/Updater may cause capacity troubles in future.\n> Users are more than welcome to manually modify their settings file\nIt's true when the user know enough about the settings.json. It's very friendly for programmers, but such \"difficult\" thing should be managed by VCC UI. Otherwise, VCC might not be needed. VCC already has a button to open settings.json and others, so I think it's enough for users who want to manually modify settings file.\nHowever sure, \"Templates\" is user documents, so I think it's good to keep it in the Documents folder, too.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
